The hinge in *Places of Learning* is not limited to built architecture alone; Ellsworth explicitly treats performed and media spaces as spatial pedagogies that create a threshold between inside/outside, private/public, institutional/everyday.

The hinge cases she develops at length in the 2005 book are:

* U.S. Holocaust Memorial Museum – the Pei-designed ramp, bridge and Hall of Witness as an architectural sequence that moves visitors across a threshold of memory and identity;
* Jane Addams Hull-House Museum – the historic settlement house front door/porch as a domestic-public hinge;
* Manhattan Children’s Museum’s Art Inside Out – the programme that produces art inside the museum and puts it literally outside on the building/street;
* Anna Deavere Smith’s verbatim performances, e.g. *Fires in the Mirror* and *Twilight: Los Angeles 1992*, which Ellsworth mobilises as performed/spatial pedagogy and a hinge for testimony, self/other;
* Bravehearts: Men in Skirts – the small-scale performative/installation practice that literalises the hinge of gendered inside/outside bodily boundaries and private dress made public.

Colleague 4’s architectural-only reading of the hinge is too narrow. Ellsworth treats Smith’s theater work as a spatial hinge precisely because it stages embodied, social thresholds. The five cases above are the canonical hinge examples in the book.

The Chicago Symphony Orchestra’s Education Center is the non-example. The Education Center at 67 E. Adams St. opened in 2019, fourteen years after *Places of Learning* was published in 2005, and it is never discussed as a hinge case in the book. It remains the anachronistic distractor.

